# Bracha 1987, "Asynchronous Byzantine Agreement Protocols"
# Information and Computation 75, 130-143 (1987)
#
# Figure 1, BPR's arms: what a (ready, v) arriving without the RECEIVED
# annotation does, and what p's (initial, v) or BESIDE arriving at a
# process that holds both does.  Two rows, in their own file because
# their inputs touch nothing in bracha87Fig1.dtc: compiled with them, dtc carries them
# down every path of the figure's dispatch for two leaves that read
# them.  Compiled alone it is a few lines, run once per event after the
# main dispatch, as bracha87Fig3.dtc runs after Figure 1's.
#
# The annotations are the caller's, riding the (ready, v) BPR already
# re-sends (bracha87Fig1.dtc, BPR's rows): ACCEPTED, the sender has
# accepted, and RECEIVED, set per recipient, the sender holds that
# recipient's accept, a (ready, v) from it carrying ACCEPTED.  A
# (ready, v) carrying ACCEPTED is recorded as its sender having
# accepted by the C, beside the ready itself, before the main dispatch;
# that record retires the re-send toward the sender.  A (ready, v)
# arriving without RECEIVED is its sender's word that it does not hold
# this process's accept.  Once this process has an accept to carry, the
# sender is armed: the next re-send goes to it, carrying ACCEPTED, and
# RECEIVED where the sender's own accept is recorded here.  A (ready, v)
# carrying RECEIVED arms nothing, so the exchange converges in one sweep
# per direction;
# a lost marked re-send is re-armed by its target's next unmarked one,
# which is the loss recovery.  Before this process accepts it has no
# accept to carry and nothing is armed: the sender's unmarked re-sends
# keep arriving, and the first after the accept arms it -- the one that
# fires the accept included, which is why "accepted" here is read after
# the main dispatch's step 3 is applied, as bracha87Fig3.dtc reads it,
# and not "have accepted" at the event's start.  An unmarked re-send is
# a duplicate (ready, v); this row runs on a duplicate, so it sees it.
#
# The arm is one re-send, not a standing bit: the C holds it per sender
# until the re-send that goes to the sender, which serves and clears
# it.  A standing bit would strand the re-send: once the sender holds
# the accept it sends nothing more here, and nothing would ever clear
# the bit.  A forged unmarked (ready, v) buys its forger one marked
# re-send per sweep, aimed at itself, displacing nothing owed to a
# correct process.
#
# Inputs:
#   "the arrival is (ready, v)"      { yes, no }
#                                    the event is the arrival of a
#                                    (ready, v); RECEIVED is read only
#                                    then
#   "carries RECEIVED"               { yes, no }
#                                    the (ready, v) carries the sender's
#                                    RECEIVED
#   "accepted"                       { yes, no }
#                                    Figure 1's, after this event: have
#                                    accepted, or step 3 taken on it
#
# Output:
#   "arm the sender"                 { yes, no }

@"arm the sender","the arrival is (ready, v)","carries RECEIVED","accepted"
yes,yes,no,yes
no,yes,no,no
no,yes,yes,
no,no,,

# The echo's arm.  HOLDING rides the echo and the ready, and both
# retire: the echo at this process's accept, the ready once the
# ACCEPTED exchange is done.  A process that comes to hold p's
# (initial, v) and BESIDE only after that has nothing left to carry
# its word, and p re-sends to it until abandon.  So p's (initial, v)
# or BESIDE arriving at a process that holds both and had echoed
# before it came is p's word that it has not heard, and arms one echo
# to p, carrying HOLDING (bracha87Fig1.dtc, BPR's retry (echo, v)).
# The arrival that completes the holding, or fires the echo, is not
# that word: the echo it fires carries HOLDING itself.  The same shape
# as the ready's arm: the message that asks is the one that was sent.
#
# Inputs:
#   "the arrival is (initial, v) or BESIDE"  { yes, no }  from p, as the
#                                    C has already checked
#   "holds both"                     { yes, no }  (initial, v) and
#                                    BESIDE from p, after this event
#   "have echoed"                    { yes, no }  Figure 1's, before this
#                                    event
#
# Output:
#   "arm the echo to p"              { yes, no }

@"arm the echo to p","the arrival is (initial, v) or BESIDE","holds both","have echoed"
yes,yes,yes,yes
no,yes,yes,no
no,yes,no,
no,no,,
